  "description": "A parametric TABLE on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A NODE, A SEGMENT OR A SPACE. In a space the table takes the space's LONGEST edge as its length direction, so a dining table in a rectangular room lands the way the room wants it without a rotation being typed.\n\nTHREE LEG STYLES, ONE TABLE. Four corner posts, a pair of trestle slabs joined by a stretcher, or a central pedestal on a foot — chosen by a ternary between three quad tables (`_isFour ? _FOUR : (_isTrestle ? _TRES : _PED)`), not by three flows. Each is a different number of rows in the same 11-column format, and everything downstream is unchanged.\n\nTHE APRON IS CONDITIONAL ON THE LEGS, not just on its checkbox: rails between the corner posts mean nothing on a pedestal, so `apron > 0 && _isFour` gates it and the false branch emits an EMPTY operand, which is the identity for `vcat`.\n\nOVERHANG IS WHY THE TOP AND THE LEGS ARE MEASURED DIFFERENTLY. The top spans the full length and width; the legs sit on an inset rectangle, so raising the overhang pulls the legs in and leaves the top where it was — which is what an overhang is.\n\nSEATS AROUND is a metric, not geometry: 0.60 m of edge per person on each of the four sides, floored — a sanity number for a dining layout that comes free from dimensions the flow already has.\n\nTHE PLAN IS DERIVED from the same quads with z dropped. Each part is its own 2D row because a grouped outline list is read EVEN-ODD: the legs sit INSIDE the top outline, and in one row they would void it into holes rather than reading as legs under a top.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: 1.60 × 0.90 m at 0.75 m high on four 0.07 m posts, 1.44 m² of top, 6 seats around.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. The top is rectangular — a round or oval table is a different body, since a quad prism cannot express a circle. Legs are vertical and square; nothing is mitred or tapered.",
